/bin/sh - what is done, what is not, and what nobody understands
================================================================

This is a reconstruction.  There is no source for the shell Micronix
shipped, so this one was read back out of that binary, mostly by
running the two side by side and comparing what came out.

Read NOTES for the reconnaissance - the addresses, the tables, and how
each thing was established.  This file is the short version of what is
left.


Where things are
----------------

    sh.c        main, the read-eval loop, the builtins, spawn
    parse.c     one statement into a pipeline; words, quoting, "( )"
    glob.c      filename patterns
    hist.c      "!", the history reference
    hash.c      the search path, remembered
    job.c       the background jobs, and kill by name
    sh.1        our man page
    regress.sh  the test

    man/man1/sh.1   THE ORIGINAL PAGE, dated 4/18/82, recovered from
                    the disk image.  A second authority beside the
                    binary, and worth reading: two mistakes here were
                    found by it and nothing else.  It is not a BETTER
                    authority - its list of special characters omits
                    ";" and includes ")", and the binary is right
                    about both - and it never mentions the positional
                    parameters at all.


How to check it
---------------

    ./regress.sh            everything
    ./regress.sh pattern    only the cases matching it
    VERBOSE=1 ./regress.sh  name every case as it passes

Ninety cases, each run through BOTH shells under the simulator with
the two answers compared.  Nothing in it records what the stock shell
is expected to say: a file of expected output would only preserve what
we believed the day we wrote it and would go on passing while being
wrong.  Three harnesses, because some behaviour only appears under
some conditions - fed a file, typed at a terminal (script(1), for
history), and run as a script with arguments (for $1).

    89 the same as the stock shell, 1 differ on purpose

A case known to answer differently is listed WITH its reason and
asserted the other way round: if one starts matching, the reason has
gone stale and the run says NOW SAME and fails.  A difference nobody
wrote a reason for is a regression.


Different on purpose
--------------------

  - dir and era are seeded ALIASES, not builtins.  In the image they
    are builtins with their own lister.  They are cp/m spellings
    rather than shell work, and as aliases they can be seen with
    alias and redefined; the table is down to what needs the shell's
    own state.  sync was a builtin too and is now cmd/sync.

  - "echo x |", a pipeline whose last stage is empty.  The image runs
    it, prints nothing and does not complain; we call it a syntax
    error.  It is not a line continuation - "echo x |" followed by
    "echo SECOND" prints SECOND there - so the trailing pipe is
    swallowed somehow and whatever echo wrote goes into a pipe nobody
    reads.  This is the one case the suite counts as differing.

  - The search tables are not trusted blindly.  The image believes
    them and loses when they are stale, which is what its page means
    by "it may be necessary to enter the command's name twice".  Here
    a name no directory claims is searched for anyway, and a path
    that will not exec sends spawn() back to rebuild and search
    before giving up.


Missing
-------

Each of these was checked against the image; none is written.

  - BARE "echo" TOGGLES COMMAND ECHOING.  The page: "The first
    command toggles the shell's command echoing feature."  It does -
    after a bare echo the image echoes every line before running it,
    "exit" included.  Ours prints a blank line.  This is -v under
    another name and the flag is already there, so it is small.

  - "prompt" WITH NO ARGUMENT says nothing in the image; ours prints
    the current prompt.  The page documents only "prompt word".

  - NOTIFICATION.  "When an asynchronous command completes or is
    abnormally terminated, the shell gives notification."  Not
    tested either way.  job.c already holds what it would need - the
    pid and the name of every background job - so this is mostly a
    matter of finding where the image prints it and what it says.

  - "wait" INTERRUPTIBLE.  "Wait may be prematurely interrupted by
    pressing the RUB-OUT or DELETE key."  Not tested either way.


Not understood
--------------

Written down because guessing at them wasted time once already.

  - WHAT RAISES "Missing (." at 0x179c.  Not an unterminated "(",
    which gives "Missing ).", and not a bare ")", which is an
    ordinary character.  Neither of the obvious ways reaches it.

  - WHY "echo `echo`" ANSWERS "No match.".  An empty substitution
    ends up in the globber somehow, and yet a substitution's output
    is NOT globbed: "echo `cat f`" with /etc/pass* in f prints
    /etc/pass* unexpanded.

  - WHICH END OF THE PATH findcmd SEARCHES FROM.  The image keeps
    three char * at 0x9741 laid out backwards, like the builtin
    table, and it has not been read which way it walks them.

  - WHETHER H5f06 really is "resume the popped input source".


Guesses
-------

Things here that are OURS rather than read, and would be worth
settling against the image:

  - ignoresigs() is called for BACKGROUND children only.  That is the
    v6 arrangement and it is what a ^C for the foreground ought to
    do, but NOTES has the two signal() calls at H4287 and not what
    guards them.

  - MAXCMD (16) and MAXARG (64).  Neither is read from the binary.

  - NHIST (16), how many lines of history are kept.  There is no
    history builtin and no message that depends on it, so nothing in
    the image says.


Known wart
----------

pipe1, the parse buffer, is 2274 bytes - sixteen commands of
sixty-four arguments each, allocated permanently because the
dimensions are compile-time constants.  It is 46% of our bss and a
line like "ls" uses one command and one argument slot.  The image
does the same job inside a 3054-byte data segment total, so it
plainly does not reserve a worst case; it presumably grows the
argument list as it reads, or points into the line buffer instead of
copying.  Nothing is wrong, and it is the obvious kilobyte to find if
one is ever wanted.

